cal=[
 ('P62C-CPS-L-025','The graph supplies the price changes and lost-trade surplus, giving $9 lost CS but only $8 of tax burden on continuing trades. A and D both describe a loss larger than the $8 burden; theory alone cannot choose the amount. Reading prices alone is also insufficient: students must distinguish the transfer from forgone surplus.'),
 ('P62E-COP-EL-031','The figure supplies the AC–AVC separation at Q=50. Students must use that difference to infer fixed cost, then spread the same fixed cost over Q=25, giving AFC=$16. The cost identity and fixed-versus-average distinction are essential; the requested AFC is not simply a displayed coordinate.'),
 ('P62B-ELAS-H-031','C and B supply the midpoint inputs. Choices C and D are both theoretically coherent revenue/elasticity pairs, so the graph is needed to choose 1.67/elastic rather than 1.00/unit elastic. The calculation must then be interpreted with the total-revenue test.'),
 ('P62B-ELAS-L-097','The actual short-run and long-run endpoints determine elasticities of about 0.23 and 0.75 and whether 90 units can be supplied at $8. B and C agree on the ordinary time-horizon rule and elasticities but disagree on procurement feasibility. Graph evidence and the distinction between responsiveness and available quantity both matter.'),
 ('P62I-OLI-EL-002','Payoffs must be read to check each firm’s best responses and to compare joint profits. A and D both posit the same Nash equilibrium but name different joint maxima. The key is B/Y with total 11 versus A/X with total 18; maximizing joint profit alone does not identify a Nash equilibrium.'),
 ('P62F-PC-H-035','The figure is needed to select Q=30 and distinguish a $300 profit from a $300 loss. A and D are both possible operating cases before the figure is seen. Students still must apply MR=MC, compare P with ATC for profit, and check AVC for short-run operation.')]
